  "reports": [
    {
      "seq": 10,
      "at": "2026-10-26T16:21:21.550Z",
      "intervention": 6,
      "distinctGoals": 1,
      "question": "What are you trying to do now? Since the last check, what, if anything, was worth doing, and what felt routine or unnecessary?",
      "answer": "I am trying to record the functioning garden and transmit its location. So far, working out that power can come from the sunny camp while water comes from heated ice was worth doing; assembling the pieces and making the two connections was straightforward. The long repeated goal output felt unnecessary, so I am filtering it for inspection."
    },
    {
      "seq": 44,
      "at": "2026-10-26T16:23:13.466Z",
      "intervention": 31,
      "distinctGoals": 6,
      "question": "What are you trying to do now? Since the last check, what, if anything, was worth doing, and what felt routine or unnecessary?",
      "answer": "I am moving on to the remaining maps after finishing six goals. Reusing saved assemblies was useful, and the two opposing light rows and moving one camera between viewpoints gave me distinct things to reason about. The ice garden repeated the earlier power-and-water solution but was quick to reuse. I had to add a real shell at the protected alcove because the place label alone did not protect the records; clearer wording there would have saved a little inspection."
    },
    {
      "seq": 88,
      "at": "2026-10-26T16:26:11.799Z",
      "intervention": 65,
      "distinctGoals": 12,
      "question": "How was the experience as a whole? What would you keep, shorten, remove or change, if anything? What led you to stop here?",
      "answer": "The campaign offered a coherent set of object-logic problems. I would keep reusable whole assemblies, automatic checking of exposure rows, actual record and sample histories, and the choice to use simple solutions. The most useful changes of problem were supplying a shaded garden remotely, covering both opposing light rows, preserving observations while moving a camera, and relaying historical records without moving their original supports. Later support and enclosure construction was often routine, but reuse kept it brief rather than making me rebuild recipes. I would shorten the repeated CLI observation output and make goal failures more specific. In particular, the protected alcove still needed an actual enclosing object, and deploy-recover required low-arch travel after spanning; the wording did not make that ordering clear to me, and the extra return trip felt like correction of an unclear condition rather than a new problem. I would clarify those requirements rather than remove the underlying mechanics. I stopped because all twelve goals were achieved and current, with no reason to prolong the completed campaign. This is an account of my AI play, not a measurement of human enjoyment or duration."
    }
  ],
